y about numbers (Number Sense) or shapes/measurement (Geometry). This is a placement judgment, not a category defined in the RMA materials themselves.\"\n  },\n  \"grades\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_1\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"supported_by_source\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_1_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_ns_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Counting, Reading, and Ordering Numbers to 100\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC4\",\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC5\",\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task A\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Count up to 100, including counting up and down from a given number.\",\n                  \"Read and write numerals up to 100.\",\n                  \"Identify the number that is 1 more or 1 less than a given number.\",\n                  \"Order numbers up to 20 from smallest to largest, and vice versa.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Numbers are everywhere — on your house, on a calendar, on a bus. Today you will practice reading numbers, finding their neighbors, and putting them in order.\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Say numbers in order, starting from any number, going up (3, 4, 5...) or down (5, 4, 3...).\",\n                  \"Step 2: To read a number, look at how it is written and say its name out loud (e.g., '9' is 'nine').\",\n                  \"Step 3: Every number has a neighbor that is 1 more (count forward once) and 1 less (count backward once).\",\n                  \"Step 4: To order numbers, find the smallest one first, then the next smallest, and so on.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Counting up means saying numbers in increasing order; counting down means saying them in decreasing order.\",\n                  \"'1 more' than a number = that number + 1. '1 less' than a number = that number − 1.\",\n                  \"'Least to greatest' means arranging numbers from smallest to largest.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What number is 1 more than 9?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"9, then the next number is 10. So 1 more than 9 is 10.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Arrange 3, 8, 9, 10, 12 from least to greatest.\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Find the smallest first (3), then 8, then 9, then 10, then 12: 3, 8, 9, 10, 12.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What number is 1 less than 9?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Count backward one step from 9.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Arrange 12, 3, 9 from least to greatest.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Which of the three is smallest? Which is biggest?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Write the number that is 1 more than 15.\",\n                  \"Write the number that is 1 less than 20.\",\n                  \"Arrange these numbers from greatest to least: 6, 2, 14, 9.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"When you line up at school by height or by number, you are ordering — just like ordering numbers from smallest to largest.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What number is 1 more than 17?\",\n                  \"Put these numbers in order from smallest to largest: 4, 1, 8.\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"You can count forward and backward, read and write numbers up to 100, find a number's neighbors (1 more/1 less), and arrange numbers from smallest to largest.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Write the numbers from 90 to 100 in order.\",\n                  \"What number is 1 more than the number that is 1 more than 8?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_ns_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"What Comes Next? (Repeating Patterns)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG1Q3-LC11\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task F\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Determine the next term(s) in a repeating pattern of shapes, numbers, or rhythms.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Have you noticed how a necklace of beads sometimes repeats the same shapes over and over? That repeating group is called a pattern.\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Look at the shapes or numbers shown, from left to right.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Find the smallest group that repeats — this is called the 'pattern unit.'\",\n                  \"Step 3: Once you know the pattern unit, you can predict what comes next by continuing the repeat.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A pattern is a sequence that repeats the same group of items in the same order.\",\n                  \"The 'pattern unit' is the smallest repeating group.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Triangle, square, rectangle, triangle, square, rectangle, ___\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The pattern unit is: triangle, square, rectangle. After rectangle, it starts over, so the answer is triangle.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Circle, half-circle, square, circle, half-circle, square, ___, ___\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The pattern unit is: circle, half-circle, square. The next two shapes are circle, half-circle.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Star, star, moon, star, star, moon, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What is the 3-item group that keeps repeating?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2, 4, 2, 4, 2, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Which two numbers keep repeating?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Red, blue, blue, red, blue, blue, ___\",\n                  \"Draw the next two shapes: square, circle, circle, square, circle, circle, ___, ___\",\n                  \"Clap, stomp, clap, stomp, clap, ___\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Patterns appear in the days of the week (they repeat every 7 days) and in songs (a chorus that repeats).\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What comes next: yellow, yellow, green, yellow, yellow, green, ___?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A pattern repeats the same group in the same order, so you can always predict what comes next.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Create your own 3-shape repeating pattern and show what the 10th shape would be.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_1_operations\",\n          \"name\": \"Operations\",\n          \"description\": \"Practice addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_op_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Putting Numbers Together (Addition)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC4\",\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC11\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task C\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate addition of numbers with sums up to 20 using objects or pictures.\",\n                  \"Describe addition as 'counting up' and 'putting together.'\",\n                  \"Add numbers with sums up to 100 without regrouping (2-digit + 1-digit, and 2-digit + 2-digit).\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you have 3 candies and your friend gives you 4 more, how many do you have now? That's addition!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Addition means combining two groups into one bigger group.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Count all the objects in the first group, then all the objects in the second group.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Put the groups together and count everything — this total is called the 'sum.'\",\n                  \"Step 4: For written addition without regrouping, add the ones digits together, then the tens digits together.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Addition combines two amounts into a total, called the sum.\",\n                  \"The '+' sign means 'add' or 'put together.'\",\n                  \"When adding 2-digit numbers, line up the ones and tens columns before adding.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"3 popsicle sticks + 4 popsicle sticks = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Put the 3 sticks and 4 sticks together and count all: 7 sticks.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"15 + 21 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Add the ones: 5 + 1 = 6. Add the tens: 1 + 2 = 3. The sum is 36.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"11 + 3 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add the ones digits first: 1 + 3.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A bundle of 10 sticks and 5 loose sticks, plus a bundle of 10 and 2 loose sticks — how many in all?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add the tens (bundles) together, then add the ones (loose sticks) together.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"12 + 5 = ?\",\n                  \"23 + 16 = ?\",\n                  \"You have 8 marbles. Your friend gives you 9 more. How many marbles do you have now?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Counting your allowance after your parents give you more money is a real-life example of addition.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"24 + 15 = ?\",\n                  \"What does the sum tell you when you add two groups together?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Addition puts two groups together to find the total, or sum. Add ones to ones and tens to tens.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"36 + 43 = ? (no regrouping needed — try it!)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_op_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Taking Away (Subtraction)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC4\",\n                \"NAG1Q1-LC5\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task D\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate subtraction involving numbers up to 20 as 'taking away.'\",\n                  \"Subtract numbers less than 100 without regrouping (2-digit − 1-digit, and 2-digit − 2-digit).\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you have 5 cookies and you eat 3, how many are left? That's subtraction!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Subtraction means taking some objects away from a group.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Start with the total amount, then remove (cross out or cover) the amount being taken away.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Count what is left — this is called the 'difference.'\",\n                  \"Step 4: For written subtraction without regrouping, subtract the ones digits, then the tens digits.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Subtraction takes an amount away from a total, leaving a difference.\",\n                  \"The '−' sign means 'take away' or 'subtract.'\",\n                  \"You cannot subtract more than what you started with.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"5 counters, cover 3 of them. How many are left?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"5 − 3 = 2. Two counters are left.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"25 − 10 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Subtract the ones: 5 − 0 = 5. Subtract the tens: 2 − 1 = 1. The difference is 15.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"12 − 2 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Subtract the ones digits: 2 − 2.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"You have 15 stickers and give 4 to a friend. How many do you have left?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Start with 15, take away 4.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"18 − 5 = ?\",\n                  \"29 − 13 = ?\",\n                  \"There are 20 birds on a wire. 6 fly away. How many birds are left?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Figuring out how much change you get back after paying for something uses subtraction.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"27 − 14 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Subtraction takes an amount away from a total, leaving a difference. Subtract ones from ones and tens from tens.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"48 − 26 = ? Show your work using tens and ones.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_1_fractions\",\n          \"name\": \"Fractions\",\n          \"description\": \"Explore parts, wholes, and equivalent fractions.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_fr_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Halves and Fourths of a Whole\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG1Q4-LC1\",\n                \"NAG1Q4-LC3\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task B\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate one-half and one-fourth as parts of a whole.\",\n                  \"Count halves and quarters.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you and your sibling share one sandwich equally, how much does each of you get? You each get one-half!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A whole is one complete object or group (like one whole pizza).\",\n                  \"Step 2: When a whole is split into 2 equal parts, each part is called one-half.\",\n                  \"Step 3: When a whole is split into 4 equal parts, each part is called one-fourth (or one-quarter).\",\n                  \"Step 4: The parts must be equal in size for the fraction name to be correct.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"One-half (1/2) means 1 out of 2 equal parts.\",\n                  \"One-fourth (1/4) means 1 out of 4 equal parts.\",\n                  \"The parts of a whole must be equal for fractions to make sense.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A square is split into 2 equal parts, and 1 part is shaded. What fraction is shaded?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"1 shaded part out of 2 equal parts = one-half.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"There are 8 breads and 4 plates. Each plate should get one-fourth of the breads. How many breads per plate?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"8 breads ÷ 4 equal groups = 2 breads on each plate.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A candy bar is split into 4 equal pieces. You eat 1 piece. What fraction did you eat?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"How many equal pieces make the whole bar?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"12 mangoes are shared equally between 2 baskets. How many mangoes are in each basket (one-half)?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Split 12 into 2 equal groups.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A pizza is cut into 2 equal slices. What fraction is 1 slice?\",\n                  \"16 crayons are shared equally in 4 boxes. How many crayons are in each box?\",\n                  \"Shade one-half of a rectangle divided into 2 equal parts.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sharing a snack equally with a friend, or splitting a chore in half, uses the idea of halves.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a whole is split into 4 equal parts, what is the name for each part?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A whole split into 2 equal parts gives halves; split into 4 equal parts gives fourths. Parts must be equal.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If 20 candies are shared equally into 4 bags (one-fourth each), how many candies are in each bag?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_1_geometry\",\n          \"name\": \"Geometry\",\n          \"description\": \"Learn shapes, space, measurement, and patterns.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_geo_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Naming Simple Shapes\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG1Q1-LC1\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task E\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Identify simple 2-dimensional shapes (triangle, rectangle, square) of different sizes and orientations.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Shapes are all around us — a book is like a rectangle, a slice of pizza is like a triangle!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A triangle has 3 sides and 3 corners.\",\n                  \"Step 2: A rectangle has 4 sides, with opposite sides equal, and 4 square corners.\",\n                  \"Step 3: A square has 4 equal sides and 4 square corners — it is a special rectangle.\",\n                  \"Step 4: A shape keeps its name even if it is turned sideways or made bigger or smaller.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Triangle = 3 sides, 3 corners.\",\n                  \"Rectangle = 4 sides (opposite sides equal), 4 square corners.\",\n                  \"Square = 4 equal sides, 4 square corners.\",\n                  \"Turning or resizing a shape does not change its name.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A set of rectangles of different sizes are shown. What are they called?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"They are all rectangles — size does not change the name.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A square cutout is shown turned sideways (like a diamond). What is it still called?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"It is still a square, because it still has 4 equal sides and 4 square corners.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"How many sides does a triangle have?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think of a slice of pizza or a roof shape.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the difference between a square and a rectangle?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the length of all 4 sides.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw a triangle, a rectangle, and a square.\",\n                  \"Sort these objects into triangle, rectangle, or square: a window, a road sign, a folder.\",\n                  \"Is a shape with 4 equal sides always a square? Explain.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"A door is shaped like a rectangle, a folded sandwich can look like a triangle, and a tile can be a square.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Name a shape with 3 sides.\",\n                  \"Name a shape with 4 equal sides.\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Triangles have 3 sides, rectangles have 4 sides with opposite sides equal, and squares have 4 equal sides.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Find 3 objects at home that are shaped like rectangles.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_geo_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Telling Time and Using a Calendar\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG1Q4-LC9\",\n                \"MGG1Q4-LC10\",\n                \"MGG1Q4-LC11\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task G\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Read and write time by the hour, half hour, and quarter hour using an analog clock.\",\n                  \"Give the days of the week and months of the year in the correct order.\",\n                  \"Determine the day and month of the year using a calendar.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"An analog clock has two hands that move to show the time — just like the hands on your favorite cartoon clock!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: The short hand shows the hour; the long hand shows the minutes.\",\n                  \"Step 2: When the long hand points to 12, it is exactly on the hour (e.g., 4:00).\",\n                  \"Step 3: When the long hand points to 6, it is half past the hour (e.g., 4:30).\",\n                  \"Step 4: The days of the week always repeat in the same order: Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Short hand = hour. Long hand = minutes.\",\n                  \"Long hand on 12 = o'clock. Long hand on 6 = half past.\",\n                  \"There are 7 days in a week and 12 months in a year, always in the same order.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"The short hand is near 4, and the long hand is on 6. What time is it?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The long hand on 6 means half past the hour, so it is 4:30.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Today is Monday. What day is tomorrow? What day was yesterday?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Tomorrow is Tuesday (the next day). Yesterday was Sunday (the day before).\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"The short hand is on 7 and the long hand is on 12. What time is it?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Long hand on 12 means exactly on the hour.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"If today is Friday, what day comes next?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think of the order of days of the week.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw clock hands to show 9:00.\",\n                  \"Name the month that comes right after June.\",\n                  \"If today is Wednesday, what was yesterday?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Reading a clock helps you know when to wake up, eat lunch, or watch your favorite show.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What time does a clock show when the long hand points to 12 and the short hand points to 8?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"An analog clock's short hand shows the hour and long hand shows the minutes. Days and months always follow the same order.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If today is the last day of a month, what is tomorrow's date likely to be?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g1_geo_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 1,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Measuring with Non-Standard Units\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG1Q2-LC1\",\n                \"MGG1Q2-LC2\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 1 Teacher's Booklet, Task H\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Measure the length of an object or the distance between two objects using non-standard units.\",\n                  \"Compare lengths and distances using non-standard units.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Before rulers were common, people measured things using objects like sticks or their own hands!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Choose a small object to use as your measuring unit (like a popsicle stick).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Line up the units end-to-end along the object you are measuring, without gaps or overlaps.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Count how many units fit — that is the length.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To compare two lengths, count the units for each and see which has more units (longer) or fewer (shorter).\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A non-standard unit is any object used to measure, like a stick, a hand span, or a paperclip.\",\n                  \"The unit must stay the same size each time you measure.\",\n                  \"The object with more units is longer; fewer units means shorter.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A ribbon lines up with 3 popsicle sticks. How long is the ribbon?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The ribbon is 3 popsicle sticks long.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Ribbon A is 3 sticks long. Ribbon B is 5 sticks long. Which is longer?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"5 is greater than 3, so Ribbon B is longer.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A pencil lines up with 6 paperclips. How long is the pencil?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Count the paperclip units laid end to end.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A table is 10 hand-spans long, and a chair is 4 hand-spans long. Which is longer?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the two numbers of hand-spans.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Measure the length of a book using your hand span. How many hand spans long is it?\",\n                  \"Compare two pencils by counting how many paperclips long each one is.\",\n                  \"Which is longer: an object that is 8 sticks long, or one that is 5 sticks long?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Before you have a ruler nearby, you can estimate the length of a table using your own footsteps.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a rope is 7 sticks long and another rope is 4 sticks long, which rope is shorter?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Non-standard units (like sticks or hand spans) can measure and compare lengths, as long as the same unit is used each time.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Measure 3 objects around you using the same non-standard unit, and list them from shortest to longest.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_2\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n      \"status\": \"supported_by_source\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_2_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_ns_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Place Value up to 1,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC5\",\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC6\",\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC10\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task A\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Read and write numerals up to 1,000.\",\n                  \"Represent numbers up to 1,000 using concrete/pictorial models and numerals.\",\n                  \"Determine the place value and value of a digit in a 3-digit number.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A 3-digit number has three 'homes' for its digits: hundreds, tens, and ones. Let's find out how to read them!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: In a 3-digit number, the digit farthest right is in the ones place.\",\n                  \"Step 2: The middle digit is in the tens place.\",\n                  \"Step 3: The digit farthest left is in the hundreds place.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To find the value of a digit, multiply the digit by what its place is worth (ones = ×1, tens = ×10, hundreds = ×100).\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Place value tells you what a digit's position means (ones, tens, hundreds).\",\n                  \"The value of a digit = digit × its place value (e.g., the 7 in 375 has a place value of tens, so its value is 7 × 10 = 70).\",\n                  \"375 is read as 'three hundred seventy-five.'\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the place value of the digit 7 in 375?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"375 has 3 in hundreds, 7 in tens, 5 in ones, so 7 is in the tens place.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the value of the digit 7 in 375?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"7 is in the tens place, so its value is 7 × 10 = 70.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Read the number 375 aloud.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Say the hundreds digit first, then 'and' the tens and ones.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the value of the digit 3 in 375?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Which place is the 3 in?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"What is the place value of the 6 in 462?\",\n                  \"What is the value of the digit 8 in 812?\",\n                  \"Write the numeral for 'six hundred fifty-two.'\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Knowing place value helps you read prices, like knowing that ₱250 means 2 hundreds, 5 tens, and 0 ones.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"In the number 519, what is the value of the digit 1?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Every digit in a number has a place (ones, tens, hundreds) and a value based on that place.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Write the largest 3-digit number you can make using the digits 4, 9, and 2 (each used once).\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_ns_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Comparing and Ordering Numbers to 1,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC6\",\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task B\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Represent numbers up to 1,000 using models and numerals.\",\n                  \"Order numbers up to 1,000 from smallest to largest, and vice versa.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Which is bigger, 857 or 954? Comparing numbers is like comparing how tall two buildings are — check the biggest part first!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: To compare two numbers, first compare the digit in the highest place value (hundreds).\",\n                  \"Step 2: If the hundreds digits are the same, compare the tens digits.\",\n                  \"Step 3: If the tens digits are also the same, compare the ones digits.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Use '>' for 'greater than' and '<' for 'less than.'\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Compare numbers starting from the leftmost (highest-value) digit.\",\n                  \"'>' means greater than; '<' means less than.\",\n                  \"A missing-digit problem can have more than one correct answer if several digits would work.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Find the missing digit that makes __54 > 857 true.\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The hundreds digit of 857 is 8. To make the number greater, the missing digit must be 9, so 954 > 857.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Compare 462 and 426. Which is greater?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Hundreds are the same (4). Compare tens: 6 > 2, so 462 > 426.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Compare 305 and 350. Which is greater?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the tens digits since the hundreds are the same.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Find a missing digit: 2__0 > 240.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What tens digit would make this number bigger than 240?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Compare 189 and 198 using > or <.\",\n                  \"Order these numbers from smallest to largest: 640, 460, 604.\",\n                  \"Find a digit that makes 7_5 greater than 725.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Comparing numbers helps you know which item costs more when shopping, or which score is higher in a game.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Which is greater: 512 or 521?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"To compare numbers, look at the highest place value first, then move right if digits are equal.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"List all the possible digits that could replace the blank in 3_6 to make it greater than 356.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_ns_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Skip Counting and Number Patterns\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC7\",\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC13\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task C\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Count by 2s, 5s, 10s, 20s, 50s, and 100s (not beyond 1,000).\",\n                  \"Determine the next terms in increasing or decreasing patterns.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Skip counting is like taking big jumps instead of little baby steps when counting!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Find the difference between the given numbers in the pattern (how much it jumps each time).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Check if the pattern is increasing (getting bigger) or decreasing (getting smaller).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Apply the same jump to find the missing number.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Skip counting means counting in equal jumps (by 2s, 5s, 10s, and so on) instead of by ones.\",\n                  \"An increasing pattern adds the same amount each time; a decreasing pattern subtracts the same amount each time.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"60, 50, 40, ___, 20\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Each number decreases by 10. After 40, the next number is 40 − 10 = 30.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"100, 120, 140, 160, ___\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Each number increases by 20. After 160, the next number is 160 + 20 = 180.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"5, 10, 15, 20, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What number is each term jumping by?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"90, 80, 70, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Is this pattern increasing or decreasing? By how much?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"2, 4, 6, 8, ___\",\n                  \"500, 400, 300, ___\",\n                  \"25, 50, 75, ___\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Skip counting by 5s helps you count coins quickly, like counting ₱5 coins.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What is the next number in this pattern: 30, 40, 50, ___?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Skip counting jumps by the same amount each time. Find the jump size, then apply it to find missing numbers.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Create a skip-counting pattern that counts down by 50s from 500 to 0.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_2_operations\",\n          \"name\": \"Operations\",\n          \"description\": \"Practice addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_op_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding 3-Digit Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC6\",\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC13\",\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC3\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task E\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Add numbers with sums up to 1,000, with or without regrouping.\",\n                  \"Solve word problems involving addition, including problems with money.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Adding bigger numbers uses the same idea as adding small numbers — just with more digits to add!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up the numbers by place value (ones under ones, tens under tens, hundreds under hundreds).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Add the ones column first. If the sum is 10 or more, write the ones digit and carry (regroup) the ten to the tens column.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Add the tens column (including any carried number), and regroup again if needed.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Add the hundreds column last.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Regrouping happens when a column's sum is 10 or more — you carry the extra ten to the next column.\",\n                  \"Always add starting from the ones column.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"152 + 234 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 2+4=6. Tens: 5+3=8. Hundreds: 1+2=3. Sum = 386.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"457 + 36 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 7+6=13, write 3 carry 1. Tens: 5+3+1=9. Hundreds: 4. Sum = 493.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"128 + 63 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add the ones first. Does it need regrouping?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Grade 2-Maya collected 128 bottles, Grade 2-Agila collected 63. How many bottles in all?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"This is the same as the addition problem above — what operation do you need?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"246 + 175 = ?\",\n                  \"389 + 47 = ?\",\n                  \"A vendor sold 215 mangoes in the morning and 128 in the afternoon. How many mangoes were sold in all?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Adding up your allowance savings across several days uses the same regrouping process.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"318 + 264 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Add numbers by place value, starting with ones, and regroup (carry) whenever a column's total is 10 or more.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"495 + 386 = ? (This one needs regrouping twice — try it!)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_op_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Subtracting 3-Digit Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC8\",\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC11\",\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC12\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task F\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate subtraction as the inverse of addition.\",\n                  \"Subtract numbers less than 1,000, with and without regrouping.\",\n                  \"Solve 1- and 2-step subtraction word problems, including money.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Subtraction can help you find your change after buying a snack — how much money is left?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up the numbers by place value.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Subtract the ones column first. If the top digit is smaller, regroup (borrow) a ten from the tens column.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Subtract the tens column, borrowing from the hundreds if needed.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Subtract the hundreds column last.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Regrouping (borrowing) happens when the top digit in a column is smaller than the bottom digit.\",\n                  \"Subtraction and addition are opposites — subtraction can 'undo' addition.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"92 − 21 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 2−1=1. Tens: 9−2=7. Difference = 71.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"396 − 78 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 6−8 needs borrowing — borrow from tens: 16−8=8. Tens becomes 8 (after borrowing), 8−7=1. Hundreds: 3. Difference = 318.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A bag costs ₱225. Carla gives ₱250. How much change?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Subtract the cost from the amount given.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"450 − 175 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Check each column — do you need to borrow?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"683 − 249 = ?\",\n                  \"A vendor had 500 eggs and sold 236. How many eggs are left?\",\n                  \"912 − 458 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Finding out how much change you should receive after a purchase is real-world subtraction.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"327 − 158 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Subtract by place value from ones to hundreds, regrouping (borrowing) whenever the top digit is smaller.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"800 − 356 = ? (Notice the top number has zeros — think carefully about borrowing!)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_op_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Introducing Multiplication\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC4\",\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC5\",\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC10\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task G\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate and write multiplication as repeated addition (equal groups, array, counting by multiples, equal jumps).\",\n                  \"Multiply using the 2, 3, 4, 5, and 10 multiplication tables.\",\n                  \"Find a missing number in a multiplication or division sentence.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you have 4 bags with 3 apples in each bag, would you rather add 3+3+3+3, or use a faster way? That faster way is multiplication!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Multiplication means adding the same number a certain number of times.\",\n                  \"Step 2: The multiplication sentence 'a × b' means 'a groups of b' (or 'b groups of a').\",\n                  \"Step 3: You can show multiplication with equal groups, an array (rows and columns), or jumps on a number line.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To find a missing number, think about what times what gives the answer.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Multiplication is repeated addition: 4 × 3 means 3+3+3+3, or 4 groups of 3.\",\n                  \"'Product' is the answer to a multiplication problem.\",\n                  \"Changing the order of the numbers being multiplied does not change the product (e.g., 3×4 = 4×3).\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"4 groups of 3 balls — how many balls in all?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"4 groups of 3 = 4 × 3 = 12 balls.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"3 × 4 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"3 groups of 4 = 4+4+4 = 12.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which multiplication sentence describes 4 groups of 3 basketballs?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Match the number of groups and the number in each group to a × b.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"___ × 10 = 50\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What number times 10 gives 50?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"2 × 5 = ?\",\n                  \"Draw 3 groups of 4 stars and write the matching multiplication sentence.\",\n                  \"___ × 2 = 16\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"If you buy 5 packs of pencils with 2 pencils in each pack, multiplication (5 × 2) tells you the total quickly.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"4 × 5 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Multiplication is a fast way to add equal groups. a × b means a groups of b.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If 6 baskets have 5 mangoes each, how many mangoes are there in all?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_op_04\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Introducing Division\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC8\",\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC9\",\n                \"NAG2Q3-LC12\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task H\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate and write division expressions as equal sharing or repeated subtraction.\",\n                  \"Divide using the 2, 3, 4, 5, and 10 multiplication tables.\",\n                  \"Solve division word problems, including problems involving money.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you have 12 candies to share equally with 3 friends, how many does each person get? That's division!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Division means splitting a total into equal groups.\",\n                  \"Step 2: 'Equal sharing' means giving out items one at a time until they run out evenly.\",\n                  \"Step 3: 'Repeated subtraction' means subtracting the group size over and over until you reach zero, and counting how many times you subtracted.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Division is the opposite of multiplication — if 3 × 4 = 12, then 12 ÷ 3 = 4 and 12 ÷ 4 = 3.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"The 'quotient' is the answer to a division problem.\",\n                  \"Division can mean 'how many in each group?' or 'how many equal groups can be made?'\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"12 balls shared equally into 3 groups — how many balls per group?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"12 ÷ 3 = 4 balls in each group.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"15 papayas, 3 per basket — how many baskets are needed?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"15 ÷ 3 = 5 baskets.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"25 ÷ 5 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"How many groups of 5 fit into 25?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"32 ÷ 4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"How many groups of 4 fit into 32?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"20 ÷ 2 = ?\",\n                  \"18 balls shared equally among 3 friends — how many balls does each friend get?\",\n                  \"40 ÷ 10 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sharing a box of 20 crayons equally among 4 classmates uses division to find out how many each person gets.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"16 ÷ 4 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Division splits a total into equal groups. It is the opposite operation of multiplication.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"45 candies are shared equally among 5 children. How many candies does each child get?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_2_fractions\",\n          \"name\": \"Fractions\",\n          \"description\": \"Explore parts, wholes, and equivalent fractions.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_fr_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Unit Fractions in Order\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q4-LC2\",\n                \"NAG2Q4-LC3\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task D\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Read and write unit fractions in fraction notation.\",\n                  \"Order unit fractions from smallest to greatest, and vice versa.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Would you rather have 1 slice of a pizza cut into 2 pieces, or 1 slice of the same pizza cut into 8 pieces? The fewer cuts, the bigger the slice!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A unit fraction always has 1 as the numerator (top number), like 1/2, 1/3, 1/4.\",\n                  \"Step 2: The denominator (bottom number) tells you how many equal parts the whole is cut into.\",\n                  \"Step 3: The more parts a whole is cut into, the smaller each part becomes.\",\n                  \"Step 4: So, for unit fractions, a bigger denominator means a smaller fraction.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A unit fraction has 1 as the numerator.\",\n                  \"For unit fractions, the larger the denominator, the smaller the fraction (1/8 < 1/2).\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which is bigger, 1/4 or 1/8?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"1/4 has a smaller denominator, so it represents a bigger piece: 1/4 > 1/8.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Arrange in order from smallest to greatest: 1/8, 1/6, 1/5, ?, 1/2\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Since the whole is being cut into fewer and fewer parts as we go, the missing fraction between 1/5 and 1/2 could be 1/4 or 1/3.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which is smaller, 1/3 or 1/6?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the denominators — which whole is cut into more pieces?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Order 1/2, 1/10, 1/5 from smallest to largest.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think about which denominator gives the smallest piece.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Which is bigger: 1/7 or 1/3?\",\n                  \"Order these from largest to smallest: 1/4, 1/9, 1/2.\",\n                  \"Write the unit fraction for 'one out of six equal parts.'\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sharing a cake among more people (bigger denominator) means each person gets a smaller piece.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Which is greater, 1/9 or 1/4?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A unit fraction has 1 as its numerator. The larger the denominator, the smaller the fraction.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Order these unit fractions from smallest to largest: 1/10, 1/2, 1/5, 1/3.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_2_geometry\",\n          \"name\": \"Geometry\",\n          \"description\": \"Learn shapes, space, measurement, and patterns.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_geo_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Shapes, Patterns, and Surfaces\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q1-LC1\",\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC13\",\n                \"NAG2Q4-LC10\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Tasks I and J\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Represent and describe circles, half circles, and quarter circles.\",\n                  \"Determine the next terms in an increasing or decreasing shape pattern.\",\n                  \"Identify the difference between straight and curved lines, and flat and curved surfaces of 3D objects.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A whole pizza is a circle. Cut it once through the middle, and each piece is a half-circle. Cut it again, and you get quarter-circles!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A circle is a round, whole shape. Cutting it in half gives two half-circles; cutting again gives quarter-circles.\",\n                  \"Step 2: In a shape pattern, look for the repeating group just like with number patterns.\",\n                  \"Step 3: A straight line goes directly from one point to another without bending; a curved line bends.\",\n                  \"Step 4: A flat surface is smooth and even (like the side of a box); a curved surface bends around (like a ball).\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Circle → half-circle → quarter-circle shows how a whole shape splits into smaller equal parts.\",\n                  \"A curved surface can roll (like a ball); a flat surface cannot.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"circle, half-circle, square, circle, half-circle, square, circle, ___, ___\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The pattern unit is circle, half-circle, square. After circle, the next two are half-circle, square.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which has a curved surface: a pyramid, a box, or a ball?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"A ball has a curved surface all around; the pyramid and box have flat surfaces.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Sort these into flat or curved surface: a can, a book, a globe.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think about whether each object can roll.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"triangle, triangle, circle, triangle, triangle, circle, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Find the repeating group of 3.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw a circle, then show how it looks cut into halves and then quarters.\",\n                  \"List 2 objects with curved surfaces and 2 objects with flat surfaces.\",\n                  \"Continue this pattern: square, square, triangle, square, square, triangle, ___, ___\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"A ball rolls because it has a curved surface, while a box stays in place because it has flat surfaces.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Does a can (like a soda can) have a flat surface, a curved surface, or both?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Circles can be split into halves and quarters. Objects can have flat surfaces, curved surfaces, or both.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Find an object at home with both flat and curved surfaces, and describe which parts are which.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g2_geo_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 2,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Measuring Length in Centimeters\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG2Q2-LC4\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 2 Teacher's Booklet, Task K\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Measure and compare the length of objects, in meters or centimeters, and distance in meters, using appropriate tools.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Now that you know how to measure with sticks, let's use a real ruler with centimeters!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up one end of the object with the 0 mark on the ruler.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Look at where the other end of the object lands on the ruler.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Read the number at that point — that is the length in centimeters (cm).\",\n                  \"Step 4: For longer distances, meters (m) are used instead of centimeters.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Centimeters (cm) measure short lengths; meters (m) measure longer distances.\",\n                  \"Always start measuring from the 0 mark on the ruler.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A pencil lines up from 0 to 15 on a ruler. How long is the pencil?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The pencil is 15 cm long.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A crayon lines up from 0 to 8 on a ruler. How long is the crayon?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The crayon is 8 cm long.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A ribbon lines up from 0 to 22 on a ruler. How long is the ribbon?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Read the number directly under the end of the ribbon.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which is longer, an object that measures 12 cm or one that measures 9 cm?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the two centimeter values.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Measure a book using a ruler and write down its length in centimeters.\",\n                  \"Compare 2 pencils by measuring each with a ruler and saying which is longer.\",\n                  \"If your arm is about 40 cm long, is that closer to the length of a spoon or a broomstick?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Tailors use centimeters to measure fabric, and carpenters use them to measure wood for furniture.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a book measures from 0 to 25 on a ruler, how long is the book?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A ruler measures exact length in centimeters, starting from the 0 mark to the other end of the object.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Measure 3 objects at home in centimeters and arrange them from shortest to longest.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_3\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n      \"status\": \"supported_by_source\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_3_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_ns_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Representing Numbers up to 10,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q1-LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task D (part 1)\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Represent numbers up to 10,000 using pictorial models (flats, longs, units, and thousands) and numerals.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Numbers can get big! Base-ten blocks help you see what a big number is really made of.\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A 'unit' block equals 1. A 'long' block equals 10 units. A 'flat' block equals 10 longs (100 units).\",\n                  \"Step 2: A 'thousands' block equals 10 flats (1,000 units).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Count how many of each block type are shown, then add up their total values.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Write the resulting number using its digits in the correct place-value positions.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Unit = 1, Long = 10, Flat = 100, Thousands block = 1,000.\",\n                  \"The total value of a picture is the sum of all the blocks' values.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"5 flats + 1 long + 2 units represent what number?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"5×100 + 1×10 + 2×1 = 500+10+2 = 512.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"4 thousands blocks + 6 flats + 2 units represent what number?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"4×1000 + 6×100 + 0×10 + 2×1 = 4,602.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"3 flats + 4 longs + 5 units — what number is this?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Multiply each block by its value, then add.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 thousands + 3 flats + 1 unit — what number is this?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Remember thousands = 1,000 each.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"6 flats + 0 longs + 8 units — what number is this?\",\n                  \"1 thousand + 9 flats + 9 longs + 9 units — what number is this?\",\n                  \"Draw a base-ten block picture for the number 234.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Understanding place value with blocks helps you understand bigger numbers, like how many students are in a whole school district.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What number is shown by 7 flats + 2 longs + 3 units?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Base-ten blocks (units, longs, flats, thousands) represent numbers by their place value, and their total tells you the number.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"What is the largest number you can make using exactly 3 flats, 3 longs, and 3 units, if you can also add thousands blocks?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_ns_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Number Patterns with Larger Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q3-LC11\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task C; RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 2\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Determine the missing term(s) in a pattern with repeating and increasing, or repeating and decreasing, components.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Patterns can now involve much bigger jumps — even jumps of 1,000!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Compare two consecutive numbers in the pattern to find how much it changes.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Check whether the pattern is increasing (going up) or decreasing (going down).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Apply the same amount of change to find the next or missing number.\",\n                  \"Step 4: For shape or bead patterns, find the repeating unit just as in earlier grades, but with more items in the unit.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"The 'common difference' is the fixed amount a number pattern increases or decreases by.\",\n                  \"A repeating pattern with shapes can have more than 3 items in its repeating unit.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"8 000, 7 000, 6 000, ___, 4 000\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Each number decreases by 1,000. After 6,000: 6,000 − 1,000 = 5,000.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"9 100, 9 105, 9 110, 9 115, ___\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Each number increases by 5. After 9,115: 9,115 + 5 = 9,120.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 000, 4 000, 6 000, ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What is the common difference?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A repeating bead pattern of triangle, heart, circle — what are the next 3 beads after triangle, heart, circle, triangle, heart, circle?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Repeat the same 3-item unit.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"10 000, 9 000, 8 000, ___\",\n                  \"3 300, 3 350, 3 400, ___\",\n                  \"500, 1 000, 1 500, ___\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Understanding number patterns helps you predict things like savings growth if you save the same amount every week.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"6 200, 6 400, 6 600, ___ — what comes next?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Find the common difference between numbers in a pattern, then apply it consistently to find missing terms.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Create a decreasing pattern that starts at 10,000 and decreases by 2,500 each time, for 4 terms.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_3_operations\",\n          \"name\": \"Operations\",\n          \"description\": \"Practice addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_op_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding Numbers up to 10,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q1-LC8\",\n                \"NAG3Q2-LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task D (part 2); RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 3\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Find the sum of numbers up to 10,000, with and without regrouping.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Adding 4-digit numbers uses the same steps as adding 3-digit numbers — just with a thousands column too!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up the numbers by place value: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Add the ones column, regrouping if the sum is 10 or more.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Add the tens, then hundreds, then thousands columns, regrouping as needed at each step.\",\n                  \"Step 4: For word problems about the 'greatest sum,' think about which digit arrangement gives the largest possible number.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Add starting from the ones column and move left, regrouping when a column totals 10 or more.\",\n                  \"To make the greatest number from given digits, place the largest digit in the highest place value.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 673 + 125 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 3+5=8. Tens: 7+2=9. Hundreds: 6+1=7. Thousands: 2. Sum = 2 798.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 325 + 2 494 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 5+4=9. Tens: 2+9=11, write 1 carry 1. Hundreds: 3+4+1=8. Thousands: 2+2=4. Sum = 4 819.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Arrange the digit cards 1, 3, 4, 6 to form the number that gives the greatest sum when added to 2,587.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"To get the greatest sum, arrange the digits from largest to smallest.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"3 456 + 2 198 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Watch for regrouping in more than one column.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"1 875 + 3 456 = ?\",\n                  \"6 042 + 1 999 = ?\",\n                  \"5 320 + 2 780 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Adding up the total number of students across several grade levels uses 4-digit addition.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"4 567 + 2 345 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Adding numbers up to 10,000 follows the same place-value steps as smaller addition, with a thousands column added.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"9 876 + 1 234 = ? Then explain which column(s) needed regrouping.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_op_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Subtracting Numbers up to 10,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q2-LC10\",\n                \"NAG3Q2-LC11\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task E; RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 10\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Subtract numbers where both numbers are less than 10,000, with and without regrouping, including word problems.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you had ₱3,000 and bought groceries, how would you find your change? Subtraction with bigger numbers!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up the numbers by place value.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Subtract starting from the ones column, borrowing from the next column whenever the top digit is smaller.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Continue subtracting through tens, hundreds, and thousands.\",\n                  \"Step 4: For 'fill in the blank' problems using equal differences, check what happens to both sides of the equation equally.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Borrowing (regrouping) moves 1 group from a higher place value into the column that needs more.\",\n                  \"If you change both numbers in a subtraction problem by the same amount, the difference stays the same (e.g., 7 500 − 1 000 = 7 000 − 500).\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"478 − 260 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 8−0=8. Tens: 7−6=1. Hundreds: 4−2=2. Difference = 218.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1 209 − 124 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Ones: 9−4=5. Tens: 0−2 needs borrowing... after regrouping, the difference is 1 085.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Maria bought groceries worth ₱2,456 and paid ₱3,000. How much change?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Subtract the cost from the amount paid.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Fill in the blank: 7 500 − 1 000 = 7 000 − ___\",\n                    \"hint\": \"If both starting numbers decrease by 500, what happens to the second number?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"6 342 − 2 178 = ?\",\n                  \"8 000 − 3 456 = ?\",\n                  \"A store had 4,500 items and sold 2,875. How many items are left?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Finding out how much money is left in a class fund after expenses uses this kind of subtraction.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"5 234 − 1 987 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Subtracting numbers up to 10,000 uses the same borrowing steps as smaller subtraction, extended to more place values.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"9 004 − 3 876 = ? (Notice the zeros — borrow carefully!)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_op_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Multiplying 2- to 4-Digit Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q3-LC8\",\n                \"NAG3Q3-LC7\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task F; RMA_4-6 Answer Key Items 1, 6, 11\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Multiply 2- to 3-digit numbers by a 1-digit number, with and without regrouping.\",\n                  \"Multiply 2- to 4-digit numbers by a number whose only non-zero digit is the leading digit (e.g., ×20, ×300), with products up to 10,000.\",\n                  \"Apply multiplication properties: order does not change the product, and any number multiplied by 1 equals itself.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Multiplying bigger numbers is like multiplying small numbers, just with more digits to work through.\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: To multiply a number by a 1-digit number, multiply each place value (ones, then tens, then hundreds) by that digit, regrouping when needed.\",\n                  \"Step 2: To multiply by a number like 20, first multiply by 2, then multiply the result by 10 (add a zero).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Remember: changing the order of numbers being multiplied does not change the answer (3×4 = 4×3).\",\n                  \"Step 4: Any number multiplied by 1 stays the same.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Multiplying by a multiple of 10 (like 20 or 300) means multiplying by the non-zero digit, then adding the zeros back.\",\n                  \"Order does not matter in multiplication (the Commutative Property).\",\n                  \"Any number × 1 = that same number (the Identity Property).\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"316 × 7 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"6×7=42, write 2 carry 4. 1×7=7+4=11, write 1 carry 1. 3×7=21+1=22. Product = 2 212.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"125 × 20 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"125 × 2 = 250. Then add a zero for the ×10: 250 × 10 = 2 500.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"321 × 4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Multiply each digit by 4, starting from the ones, and regroup as needed.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"If N is any number, what gives the same answer as 8 × N?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Which multiplication property applies here?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"234 × 3 = ?\",\n                  \"150 × 40 = ?\",\n                  \"618 × 5 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"If a jeepney can carry 20 passengers and there are 15 trips in a day, multiplication finds the total passengers.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"407 × 6 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Multiply by place value, regroup as needed, and remember order and identity properties can make problems easier.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"1 25 × 300 = ? (Think about how many zeros to add.)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_op_04\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Dividing 2- to 4-Digit Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NCG3Q4-LC4\",\n                \"NAG3Q4-LC2\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task G; RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 12\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Divide 2- to 3-digit numbers by a 1-digit number, with and without remainder.\",\n                  \"Divide 2- to 4-digit numbers by 10, 100, and 1,000.\",\n                  \"Find a missing number in a division sentence.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Sometimes when you share things equally, there's a little bit left over — that's called a remainder!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Ask how many times the divisor fits into the number being divided.\",\n                  \"Step 2: If it doesn't divide evenly, the amount left over is the remainder.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To divide by 10, 100, or 1,000, remove that many zeros from the end of the number.\",\n                  \"Step 4: For missing-number problems, think about the related multiplication fact.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A remainder is the amount left over when a number cannot be divided evenly.\",\n                  \"Dividing by 10, 100, or 1,000 is the same as removing 1, 2, or 3 zeros.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"18 ÷ 6 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"6 fits into 18 exactly 3 times, no remainder: 18 ÷ 6 = 3.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"38 ÷ 9 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"9 × 4 = 36, which leaves 2 left over: 38 ÷ 9 = 4 remainder 2.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"168 mangoes shared equally in 8 baskets — how many mangoes per basket?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think: how many groups of 8 fit into 168?\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"56 ÷ ___ = 7\",\n                    \"hint\": \"What number times 7 gives 56?\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"45 ÷ 7 = ?\",\n                  \"3 400 ÷ 100 = ?\",\n                  \"72 ÷ 8 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Splitting a bag of 50 candies equally among 6 friends and figuring out the leftover uses division with remainder.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"29 ÷ 5 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Division finds how many equal groups fit into a total, and any amount that doesn't fit evenly is the remainder.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"7 500 ÷ 1 000 = ? and 63 ÷ 8 = ? (with remainder)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_op_05\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Collecting and Counting Data\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q3-LC1\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 4 (labeled Grade 3, Quarter 3)\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Collect data from simple experiments with a small number of possible outcomes (e.g., tossing a coin or rolling a die).\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you flip a coin 10 times, will you always get exactly 5 heads and 5 tails? Let's find out by counting!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Record the result of each trial (like each coin toss) in order.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Use tally marks or a table to count how many times each outcome happened.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Add up the tallies for the outcome you are asked about.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A trial is one single try, like one coin toss.\",\n                  \"Recording results in a table makes counting outcomes easier and more accurate.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A coin is tossed 10 times: Head, Head, Tail, Tail, Head, Tail, Tail, Head, Head, Head. How many heads?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Counting all the 'Head' results: 1st, 2nd, 5th, 8th, 9th, 10th = 6 heads.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"If the same 10 tosses had 6 heads, how many tails were there?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"10 total tosses minus the number of heads.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A die is rolled 6 times and lands on: 3, 5, 3, 2, 3, 6. How many times did it land on 3?\",\n                  \"Flip a real or imaginary coin 10 times and tally your results.\",\n                  \"Out of 20 tosses, 12 were heads. How many were tails?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Weather reporters collect daily rain/no-rain data over a month to see patterns, just like tallying coin tosses.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a coin is tossed 8 times and lands on heads 5 times, how many times did it land on tails?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Collecting data means recording and tallying results from repeated trials to count outcomes accurately.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Design your own 10-trial experiment (like flipping a coin) and predict how many of each outcome you expect before you try it.\"\n                ]\n              },\n              \"moduleMappingNote\": \"The RMA source materials do not define a separate 'Data & Probability' module in the Mathalino Learn section. This lesson was placed in Operations because the RMA item requires the learner to compute or compare quantities (tallies, totals, differences, or chance expressed as a fraction) rather than reason abstractly about numbers (Number Sense) or shapes/measurement (Geometry). This is a placement judgment, not a category defined in the RMA materials themselves.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_3_fractions\",\n          \"name\": \"Fractions\",\n          \"description\": \"Explore parts, wholes, and equivalent fractions.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_fr_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding and Subtracting Similar Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q4-LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task A; RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 7\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Add and subtract similar fractions (same denominator) using models.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If a pizza is cut into 8 equal slices, and you eat 3 slices, how much of the pizza is left?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Similar fractions have the same denominator (bottom number).\",\n                  \"Step 2: To add similar fractions, add only the numerators (top numbers) and keep the denominator the same.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To subtract similar fractions, subtract only the numerators and keep the denominator the same.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Check if your answer can be simplified.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Similar fractions share the same denominator.\",\n                  \"Add or subtract only the numerators when denominators match; the denominator stays the same.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1/8 shaded + 2/8 shaded = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Add the numerators: 1+2=3. Keep the denominator: 3/8.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A loaf of bread minus 3/8 given away — how much is left?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"1 whole = 8/8. 8/8 − 3/8 = 5/8 of the bread is left.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2/6 + 3/6 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add only the numerators.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"7/9 − 4/9 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Subtract only the numerators.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"3/10 + 4/10 = ?\",\n                  \"5/7 − 2/7 = ?\",\n                  \"2/5 + 2/5 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sharing a chocolate bar cut into equal pieces and tracking how many pieces are eaten uses similar fractions.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"4/8 + 3/8 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"For similar fractions, add or subtract the numerators only, and keep the denominator the same.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"6/8 − 5/8 = ? Can this answer be simplified further?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_fr_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Fractions Equal to and Greater Than One\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"NAG3Q4-LC7\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 8\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Represent fractions that are equal to one and greater than one using models.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Can a fraction be bigger than a whole pizza? Yes — if you have more than one whole pizza cut into equal slices!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A fraction is equal to 1 whole when the numerator and denominator are the same (e.g., 2/2, 5/5).\",\n                  \"Step 2: A fraction is greater than 1 whole when the numerator is bigger than the denominator (e.g., 3/2).\",\n                  \"Step 3: To shade a fraction greater than 1, you need more than one whole region, each divided into equal parts.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Numerator = denominator means the fraction equals 1 whole.\",\n                  \"Numerator > denominator means the fraction is greater than 1 whole.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Shade 3/2 of a region using 5 whole regions each split into halves.\",\n                    \"solution\": \"3/2 means 3 half-pieces. Shade 1 whole region (2 halves) plus 1 more half from another region.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Is 4/4 equal to, less than, or greater than 1 whole?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the numerator and denominator.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Is 5/3 equal to, less than, or greater than 1 whole?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare the numerator and denominator.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Shade 5/4 using whole regions split into fourths.\",\n                  \"Is 7/7 equal to 1 whole? Explain.\",\n                  \"Is 9/5 greater than 1 whole? Explain.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"If you need 1 and a half pizzas for a party, you are using a fraction greater than 1 (3/2 pizzas).\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Is 6/5 equal to, less than, or greater than 1 whole?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A fraction equals 1 whole when numerator = denominator, and is greater than 1 whole when numerator > denominator.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Show 7/3 using whole regions split into thirds. How many whole regions do you need at least?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_3_geometry\",\n          \"name\": \"Geometry\",\n          \"description\": \"Learn shapes, space, measurement, and patterns.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_geo_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Measuring Mass in Grams\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG3Q2-LC1\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task B\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Measure mass in grams, kilograms, and/or milligrams using appropriate measuring tools.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A balance scale tips to the heavier side — just like a seesaw!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A balance scale is level when both sides have the same mass.\",\n                  \"Step 2: If known weights (like cubes) balance an object, the object's mass equals the total mass of the weights.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To find total mass, multiply the number of equal weights by the mass of each weight.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Mass is measured in grams (g), kilograms (kg), or milligrams (mg).\",\n                  \"A balanced scale means both sides have equal mass.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A bag balances against 5 cubes, each with a mass of 10 grams. What is the mass of the bag?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"5 × 10 g = 50 g. The bag has a mass of 50 grams.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A toy balances against 8 cubes, each 5 grams. What is the toy's mass?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Multiply the number of cubes by the mass of each cube.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A book balances against 12 cubes, each 20 grams. What is the book's mass?\",\n                  \"If a fruit has a mass of 150 grams, and each weight cube is 25 grams, how many cubes balance it?\",\n                  \"Order these masses from lightest to heaviest: 250 g, 1 kg, 500 g.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Bakers use grams to measure ingredients like flour and sugar precisely for recipes.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"A box balances against 6 cubes, each 15 grams. What is the box's mass?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A balance scale shows equal mass on both sides; multiply the number of weights by their individual mass to find total mass.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If 1 kilogram = 1,000 grams, how many grams is 3.5 kilograms?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_geo_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Parallel, Intersecting, and Perpendicular Lines\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG3Q1-LC6\",\n                \"MGG3Q1-LC7\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"Grade 3 Teacher's Booklet, Task H\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Recognize and draw parallel, intersecting, and perpendicular lines.\",\n                  \"Identify and draw line segments of equal length using a ruler.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Look at a ladder — the two long side rails never touch. Now look at a plus sign (+) — the two lines cross in the middle!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Parallel lines are always the same distance apart and never meet, no matter how far they extend.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Intersecting lines cross each other at one point.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Perpendicular lines are a special type of intersecting lines that cross to form a square (90°) corner.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To check if line segments are equal, measure each one with a ruler and compare.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Parallel lines never meet and stay the same distance apart.\",\n                  \"Intersecting lines cross at one point.\",\n                  \"Perpendicular lines intersect to form a right angle (square corner).\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which edges of a rectangular box are parallel?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"The top and bottom edges of the same face are parallel — they never meet.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which edges of a rectangular box are perpendicular?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Two edges that meet at a corner and form a square angle, like the side edge and the bottom edge.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Trace two edges of a box that intersect.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Look for two edges that share a corner point.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Trace two line segments in a box that appear to be the same length.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Use a ruler to measure and compare.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw two parallel lines.\",\n                  \"Draw two perpendicular lines.\",\n                  \"Name 2 objects around you that show parallel lines (like railroad tracks).\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Railroad tracks are parallel lines, and the corner of a picture frame shows perpendicular lines.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What do we call two lines that cross to form a square corner?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Parallel lines never meet, intersecting lines cross at a point, and perpendicular lines cross to form a right angle.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Find 3 examples of perpendicular lines in your classroom or home.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_geo_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Comparing Areas of Squares and Rectangles\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG3Q1-LC4\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 9\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Solve problems involving the areas of squares and rectangles.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you cut a rectangle and rearrange the pieces into a square, does the amount of space it covers change?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Area is the amount of flat space a shape covers.\",\n                  \"Step 2: When you cut a shape and rearrange the same pieces (without removing or adding anything), the total area stays the same.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Compare the resulting shape's area to the original by thinking about whether any material was added or lost.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Cutting and rearranging the same material does not change the total area.\",\n                  \"Area problems can be solved by reasoning about the pieces, not just by calculating with numbers.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A rectangular cutout is cut into two equal parts to form a square cutout. How does the square's area compare to the rectangle's?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Since no material was added or removed — only rearranged — the square's area is the same as the original rectangle's area.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"If you cut a square piece of paper in half and rearrange the two halves, does the total area of the new shape change?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think about whether the paper's amount changed.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A rectangle is cut into 4 equal strips and rearranged into a new shape. Compare the areas.\",\n                  \"Explain, in your own words, why cutting and rearranging doesn't change total area.\",\n                  \"If a rectangle has an area of 24 square units and is rearranged into a different shape, what is the new shape's area?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Tailors rearrange fabric pieces to cut clothing patterns without wasting fabric, relying on this same idea about area.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a shape is cut and rearranged without adding or removing pieces, does its area increase, decrease, or stay the same?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Cutting a shape into pieces and rearranging them does not change the total area, as long as nothing is added or removed.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Draw a rectangle, cut it (on paper) into 2 triangles, and explain why both triangles together still equal the rectangle's original area.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g3_geo_04\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 3,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Sliding Shapes (Translation)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"MGG3Q4-LC9\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key Item 5 (labeled Grade 3, Quarter 4)\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Describe and draw the effect of a two-direction, multi-step slide (translation) on a basic shape.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Imagine sliding a book across a table — it moves without turning or flipping. That's called a translation!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A slide (translation) moves a shape in a straight direction without rotating or flipping it.\",\n                  \"Step 2: A slide can happen in more than one direction, like 'right' and 'down' together.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Count the grid squares for each direction and move every point of the shape the same amount.\",\n                  \"Step 4: The shape's size and orientation stay exactly the same after sliding — only its position changes.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A translation (slide) moves a shape without turning or flipping it.\",\n                  \"Every point of the shape moves the same distance and direction.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Slide a triangle 2 units to the right and 3 units down. Where does it land?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Move every corner of the triangle 2 squares right, then 3 squares down, keeping the same size and shape.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Slide a square 3 units to the right and 1 unit down on a grid.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Move each corner point the same amount.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Slide a rectangle 4 units left and 2 units up. Describe its new position.\",\n                  \"If a shape's corner started at grid position (2,3) and slides 3 right and 1 down, where is the new corner?\",\n                  \"Draw a shape on grid paper, then slide it 2 right and 2 down.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Moving a game piece across a board game grid is an example of a translation (slide).\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What happens to the size and shape of a figure after it is translated (slid)?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A translation slides a shape in one or more directions without changing its size, shape, or orientation.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If a shape is slid 5 right and 5 down, then slid again 2 left and 3 up, where does it end up compared to its starting position?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_4\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n      \"status\": \"supported_by_source\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_4_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [],\n          \"note\": \"No RMA item in the attached Grade 4-6 answer key was explicitly labeled as a Grade 4 'Number Sense' (place value / number representation) competency. The Grade 4 items that involve numbers (addition and subtraction of large numbers) are computation skills and are listed under Operations instead. This gap is documented here rather than inventing a Grade 4 Number Sense lesson.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_4_operations\",\n          \"name\": \"Operations\",\n          \"description\": \"Practice addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_op_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding and Subtracting Numbers up to 1,000,000\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 LC13\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 15 and 19\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Add and subtract numbers where both numbers are less than 1,000,000, with and without regrouping.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"From thousands, we now work with numbers up to a million — like counting a whole city's population!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Line up very large numbers carefully by place value, from ones up through hundred-thousands.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Add or subtract column by column, starting from the ones place.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Regroup (carry for addition, borrow for subtraction) whenever needed, watching zeros carefully.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Check your answer by estimating — does it seem like a reasonable size?\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"The same regrouping rules from smaller numbers apply to 6-digit numbers — there are just more columns.\",\n                  \"Zeros in the number being subtracted from require extra careful borrowing across several columns.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"900 000 − 87 246 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Borrowing across the zeros: 900,000 − 87,246 = 812,754.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"452 042 + 38 594 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Adding by place value with regrouping: 452,042 + 38,594 = 490,636.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"300 000 − 45 678 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Watch the zeros carefully when borrowing.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"215 300 + 84 750 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Line up the digits by place value before adding.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"725 000 − 198 450 = ?\",\n                  \"356 210 + 289 875 = ?\",\n                  \"1 000 000 − 456 789 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Government agencies add and subtract large population or budget figures using this same process.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"600 000 − 123 456 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Adding and subtracting numbers up to 1,000,000 uses the same place-value process as smaller numbers, with careful attention to zeros.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"500 005 − 249 876 = ? Explain each regrouping step.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_op_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Dividing 3- to 4-Digit Numbers by a 1-Digit Number\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q2 LC4a\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 14\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Divide 3- to 4-digit numbers by 1-digit numbers, with and without regrouping.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If a school collects money and needs to split it fairly among all grade levels, division makes it fair for everyone!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Start dividing from the leftmost digit of the number being divided.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Find how many times the divisor fits into that part of the number.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Write the result, subtract, bring down the next digit, and repeat.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Continue until every digit has been brought down and divided.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Long division works digit by digit, from left to right.\",\n                  \"If there's a remainder at the very end, the division did not come out evenly.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"₱8,700 divided equally among 6 grade levels — how much per grade level?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"8,700 ÷ 6 = 1,450. Each grade level receives ₱1,450.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 448 ÷ 4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Divide starting from the leftmost digit and work through each place.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"3 654 ÷ 3 = ?\",\n                  \"5 040 ÷ 7 = ?\",\n                  \"A school raised ₱9,600 and wants to divide it equally among 8 classrooms. How much does each classroom get?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Splitting a fundraising total equally among several school clubs uses this kind of division.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"4 928 ÷ 8 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Divide 3- to 4-digit numbers by working through each place value from left to right, one digit at a time.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"7 236 ÷ 6 = ? Show each step of your long division.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_op_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Reading and Comparing Data in Graphs\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q4 LC3\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 27\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Interpret data presented in tabular form or in a single line graph.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A line graph is like a picture story of numbers changing over time — did something go up, down, or stay the same?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Look at what the two axes of the graph represent (for example, months and number of books).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Find the value for each point on the graph.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To find the largest decrease, compare each pair of consecutive points and find which pair has the biggest drop.\",\n                  \"Step 4: A steeper downward line means a bigger decrease; a steeper upward line means a bigger increase.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A line graph shows how a value changes across categories (often time periods).\",\n                  \"The steepest drop between two points shows the largest decrease.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A line graph of books read shows: August 30, September 20, October 25, November 19, December 15. Which months had the largest decrease?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Compare each drop: Aug→Sep (−10), Sep→Oct (+5), Oct→Nov (−6), Nov→Dec (−4). The largest decrease is August to September.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"If a graph shows sales of 50, 45, 60, 30 over four weeks, between which two weeks was the largest decrease?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Calculate the difference between each pair of consecutive weeks.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A graph shows temperatures of 30°C, 32°C, 28°C, 31°C over 4 days. Between which two days was the largest decrease?\",\n                  \"Explain what it means when a line graph goes downward.\",\n                  \"Create a simple line graph of your own weekly savings for 4 weeks and find the largest increase.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Doctors use line graphs to track a patient's temperature or weight over time and spot sudden changes.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a value goes from 40 to 25, how much did it decrease?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A line graph shows how values change over time; comparing consecutive points reveals the largest increase or decrease.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Given the points 10, 40, 15, 45, 20 across 5 periods, which two consecutive periods show the greatest increase?\"\n                ]\n              },\n              \"moduleMappingNote\": \"The RMA source materials do not define a separate 'Data & Probability' module in the Mathalino Learn section. This lesson was placed in Operations because the RMA item requires the learner to compute or compare quantities (tallies, totals, differences, or chance expressed as a fraction) rather than reason abstractly about numbers (Number Sense) or shapes/measurement (Geometry). This is a placement judgment, not a category defined in the RMA materials themselves.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_4_fractions\",\n          \"name\": \"Fractions\",\n          \"description\": \"Explore parts, wholes, and equivalent fractions.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_fr_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding Dissimilar Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q3 LC10a\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 21\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Add dissimilar fractions (two proper fractions with different denominators), expressing the answer in simplest form.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"What if two fractions don't have the same denominator? We need to make them match first before adding!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Dissimilar fractions have different denominators, so they cannot be added directly.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Find a common denominator — a number both denominators can divide into evenly.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Rewrite each fraction using the common denominator.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Add the numerators, keep the common denominator, then simplify the answer if possible.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A common denominator is a shared multiple of both denominators.\",\n                  \"Always simplify (reduce) your final fraction answer if possible.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"4/6 + 3/9 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Common denominator of 6 and 9 is 18. 4/6 = 12/18, 3/9 = 6/18. 12/18 + 6/18 = 18/18 = 1.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1/2 + 1/4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Find a common denominator for 2 and 4 first.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"2/3 + 1/6 = ?\",\n                  \"3/4 + 1/8 = ?\",\n                  \"1/5 + 2/10 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"If you eat 1/3 of a cake on Monday and 1/6 on Tuesday, adding dissimilar fractions tells you the total you ate.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"1/3 + 1/9 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"To add dissimilar fractions, rewrite them with a common denominator, add the numerators, then simplify.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"2/5 + 3/10 = ? Simplify your final answer completely.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_fr_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Adding Mixed Numbers with Dissimilar Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q3 LC10b\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 25\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Add dissimilar fractions in the form of two mixed numbers.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A mixed number has a whole number and a fraction together, like 1 and a half cups of flour!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Add the whole numbers together first.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Find a common denominator for the fraction parts.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Add the fraction parts using the common denominator.\",\n                  \"Step 4: If the fraction part is greater than or equal to 1, regroup it into the whole number part.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A mixed number has a whole number part and a fraction part.\",\n                  \"If adding the fraction parts gives 1 or more, carry that extra whole into the whole-number part.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1 5/6 m + 2 1/3 m = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Add wholes: 1+2=3. Common denominator for 6 and 3 is 6: 5/6 + 2/6 = 7/6, which is 1 1/6. So 3 + 1 1/6 = 4 1/6 m.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"2 1/2 + 1 1/4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add the whole numbers first, then find a common denominator for the fractions.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"3 1/3 + 2 1/6 = ?\",\n                  \"1 3/4 + 2 1/2 = ?\",\n                  \"4 2/5 + 1 3/10 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"A carpenter adding two board lengths, like 2 1/2 meters and 1 1/4 meters, uses mixed-number addition.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"2 1/4 + 1 1/2 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Add the whole numbers and fraction parts of mixed numbers separately, then regroup if the fraction sum reaches 1 or more.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"3 5/6 + 2 3/4 = ? (This one needs regrouping into a bigger whole number!)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_4_geometry\",\n          \"name\": \"Geometry\",\n          \"description\": \"Learn shapes, space, measurement, and patterns.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_geo_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Types of Angles\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 LC1\",\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 LC2\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 13 and 26\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate different angles (right, acute, obtuse) using models.\",\n                  \"Measure and draw angles using a protractor.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Open a book just a little, and you make a sharp angle. Open it all the way flat, and the angle is very wide!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A right angle looks like a perfect square corner (90°).\",\n                  \"Step 2: An acute angle is smaller/sharper than a right angle (less than 90°).\",\n                  \"Step 3: An obtuse angle is wider/more open than a right angle (between 90° and 180°).\",\n                  \"Step 4: A protractor measures the exact number of degrees in an angle — line up its base with one side of the angle and read where the other side points.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Right angle = exactly 90°. Acute angle = less than 90°. Obtuse angle = more than 90° but less than 180°.\",\n                  \"A protractor is the tool used to measure angles in degrees.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which of these objects shows an obtuse angle: scissors opened wide, a table's square corner, a hand fan?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"A hand fan opened wide shows an obtuse angle, since it is wider than a square corner.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Draw a 140-degree angle from an already-drawn side.\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Line up the protractor's base along the drawn side, find the 140° mark, and draw the second side to that mark.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Is the angle formed by a fully opened book (180°, flat) a right, acute, or obtuse angle?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare it to 90°.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Use a protractor to measure the corner of this page. What angle is it?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Book and page corners are usually right angles.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw an acute angle and label it.\",\n                  \"Draw an obtuse angle and label it.\",\n                  \"Use a protractor to draw a 60-degree angle.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Carpenters use a protractor-like tool to make sure furniture corners are exactly right angles.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Is a 45° angle acute, right, or obtuse?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Angles are classified as right (90°), acute (less than 90°), or obtuse (between 90° and 180°), and measured exactly with a protractor.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Draw a 110-degree angle using a protractor, then classify it as acute, right, or obtuse.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_geo_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Properties of Triangles and Quadrilaterals\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 MG LC3\",\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 MG LC4\",\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 MG LC5\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 17\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Draw and state the properties of triangles and quadrilaterals.\",\n                  \"Classify triangles and quadrilaterals according to sides and angles.\",\n                  \"Differentiate different quadrilaterals.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Cutting a square along a diagonal line creates two new shapes — but what are they called, and why?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A quadrilateral is any shape with 4 sides. Different quadrilaterals (square, rectangle, parallelogram) have different rules about their sides and angles.\",\n                  \"Step 2: A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel, equal sides, and opposite angles that are equal.\",\n                  \"Step 3: When a square is cut along its diagonal, the resulting shapes may be a different quadrilateral, like a parallelogram, because certain sides remain equal and parallel.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To explain your answer, describe which sides are equal, which are parallel, and which angles are equal.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A quadrilateral has 4 sides.\",\n                  \"A parallelogram has 2 pairs of parallel, equal sides and equal opposite angles.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Square ABCD is cut along diagonal AC, forming two shapes joined together. What quadrilateral is formed?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"A parallelogram, because its two pairs of sides remain parallel and equal, and opposite angles are equal.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What makes a shape a parallelogram instead of just any quadrilateral?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think about parallel sides and equal angles.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"List two properties of a square.\",\n                  \"List two properties of a rectangle that are different from a square.\",\n                  \"Explain why a parallelogram is still a quadrilateral.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Recognizing quadrilateral properties helps architects and engineers design stable shapes for buildings and bridges.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What are two properties that define a parallelogram?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Quadrilaterals are classified by their sides and angles; a parallelogram has two pairs of parallel, equal sides and equal opposite angles.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"If you cut a rectangle along its diagonal, what shape(s) result, and how do you know?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_geo_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Perimeter of Quadrilaterals and Composite Figures\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 LC6\",\n                \"Grade 4 Q1 LC7\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 20 and 23\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Find the perimeter of quadrilaterals that are not squares or rectangles.\",\n                  \"Find the perimeter of composite figures made of triangles and quadrilaterals.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you walked all the way around the edge of your classroom, the total distance you walked is the perimeter!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Perimeter is the total distance around the outside edge of a shape.\",\n                  \"Step 2: For any polygon, add up the lengths of all its sides.\",\n                  \"Step 3: For composite figures (two shapes joined together), only count the outer edges — do not count the edges where the shapes overlap or join inside.\",\n                  \"Step 4: If two joined shapes have different overlapping edge lengths, their combined perimeters will not match exactly.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Perimeter = the sum of all outer side lengths of a shape.\",\n                  \"For composite figures, exclude any internal (joined/overlapping) edges from the perimeter count.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A parallelogram has sides that add up to a certain total. Which other quadrilateral has the same perimeter?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Add up all 4 sides of the parallelogram, then check which other shape's side lengths add up to the same total.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Two cutouts (rectangle and triangle) are joined to form 2 figures. Are their perimeters the same?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"No — if the overlapping (shared) sides are different lengths in each figure, the resulting perimeters will differ.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A quadrilateral has sides 5 cm, 5 cm, 3 cm, and 3 cm. What is its perimeter?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add all four side lengths together.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A triangle has sides 4 cm, 6 cm, and 5 cm. What is its perimeter?\",\n                  \"A composite figure is made of a square (4 cm sides) joined to a triangle. If the joined edge is not counted, and the triangle's other two sides are 5 cm each, what is the total perimeter?\",\n                  \"Explain in your own words why overlapping edges are not counted in a composite figure's perimeter.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Fencing a garden shaped like an irregular quadrilateral requires calculating its perimeter to know how much fence to buy.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"A shape has sides 6 cm, 4 cm, 6 cm, and 4 cm. What is its perimeter?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Perimeter is the sum of all outer sides of a shape; for composite figures, only outer (non-shared) edges are counted.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Two shapes are joined with an overlapping edge of 3 cm on one figure and 4 cm on another otherwise identical figure. Explain why their total perimeters differ.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_geo_04\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Reflections (Flips)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q3 LC14\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 22\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Draw the image of an object after applying a reflection with respect to a line, including glide reflection.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Look in a mirror — your reflection matches you exactly, but flipped! That's exactly how shape reflections work.\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A reflection flips a shape over a line (called the line of reflection), like a mirror.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Every point on the shape has a matching point on the other side of the line, at the same distance from the line.\",\n                  \"Step 3: The reflected shape is the same size as the original, but faces the opposite direction.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To check if two shapes show a true reflection, see if one is a mirror image of the other across the line shown.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A reflection flips a shape across a line, creating a mirror image.\",\n                  \"The reflected image is the same size as the original shape, just facing the opposite way.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which of 4 shape pairs does NOT show a reflection (flip)?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Check each pair against its line — the pair that is not a true mirror image (for example, it's rotated instead of flipped) is the one that does NOT show a reflection.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Draw a triangle and a vertical line next to it. Then draw its reflection.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Each point of the reflected triangle should be the same distance from the line as the original point, but on the other side.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Draw a simple shape and reflect it over a horizontal line.\",\n                  \"Explain how a reflection is different from a slide (translation).\",\n                  \"Look at your own reflection in a mirror — describe one way it matches you and one way it's reversed.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Reflections are used in designing butterfly wings, building facades, and other symmetric designs.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Does a reflected shape change size? Does it change direction?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A reflection flips a shape across a line, producing a same-sized mirror image facing the opposite direction.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Draw a shape that looks the same before and after being reflected across a certain line. What is special about this shape?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g4_geo_05\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 4,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Converting Units of Measurement\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 4 Q2 LC10\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 16, 18, and 24\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Solve problems involving conversion of units of length, mass, capacity, and time, including elapsed time in hours and minutes.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Have you ever needed to know if 1,000 mL of juice fits in a 1-liter bottle? Converting units helps answer questions like this!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Different units measure the same kind of quantity (e.g., grams and kilograms both measure mass; milliliters and liters both measure capacity).\",\n                  \"Step 2: 1 kilogram = 1,000 grams. 1 liter = 1,000 milliliters.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To convert or combine, make sure all amounts are in the same unit before adding or comparing.\",\n                  \"Step 4: For elapsed time, count forward from the starting time by the amount of time that passed.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"1 kg = 1,000 g. 1 L = 1,000 mL.\",\n                  \"Elapsed time = ending time − starting time, or count forward from the start.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which has the same mass as one-half kilogram of flour?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"One-half kg = 500 g, so a 500 g bag of sugar has the same mass.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Tito leaves at 6:05 A.M. and walks for half an hour. What time does he arrive?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"6:05 + 30 minutes = 6:35 A.M.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Aling Lea mixes 1,000 mL of water and 750 mL of juice. What is the total, and what size container is needed?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Add the two capacities together, then think about which container size (in liters) is large enough.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A class starts at 1:15 P.M. and ends 45 minutes later. What time does it end?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Count forward 45 minutes from 1:15.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"How many grams is 2 kilograms?\",\n                  \"How many milliliters is 3 liters?\",\n                  \"A trip starts at 9:40 A.M. and takes 1 hour 20 minutes. What time does it end?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Cooking recipes often require converting between milliliters and liters, or grams and kilograms.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"How many grams are in 1.5 kilograms?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Converting units means expressing the same quantity using a related unit (like grams to kilograms), using 1,000 as the key conversion number for both mass and capacity.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"A movie starts at 2:50 P.M. and runs for 2 hours 15 minutes. What time does it end?\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_5\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n      \"status\": \"supported_by_source\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_5_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_ns_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"number_sense\",\n              \"title\": \"Place Value in Decimal Numbers\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q2 LC4b\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 34\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Determine the value of a digit in a given decimal number.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"After the decimal point, numbers get smaller and smaller — tenths, hundredths, thousandths!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: In a decimal number, the digit right after the decimal point is in the tenths place.\",\n                  \"Step 2: The next digit is in the hundredths place, then thousandths, and so on.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To find the value of a digit, multiply it by its place value (tenths = ÷10, hundredths = ÷100).\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"After the decimal point: 1st digit = tenths, 2nd digit = hundredths, 3rd digit = thousandths.\",\n                  \"281.379: the 3 is in tenths, 7 is in hundredths, 9 is in thousandths.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the value of the digit 7 in 281.379?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"7 is the second digit after the decimal point, so it is in the hundredths place: its value is 7 hundredths (0.07).\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the value of the digit 4 in 15.482?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Count how many places after the decimal point the 4 is.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"What is the value of the digit 9 in 63.912?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Identify which decimal place the 9 occupies.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"What is the value of the digit 5 in 7.253?\",\n                  \"What is the value of the digit 8 in 0.089?\",\n                  \"Write a decimal number where the digit 6 is in the hundredths place.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Reading prices like ₱45.75 requires understanding that the .75 means 75 hundredths of a peso.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What is the value of the digit 3 in 9.638?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Decimal place values (tenths, hundredths, thousandths) work the same way as whole-number place values, but represent parts smaller than 1.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"In the number 502.146, name the place value of every digit.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_5_operations\",\n          \"name\": \"Operations\",\n          \"description\": \"Practice addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_op_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Multi-Step Problems with Decimals and Money\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q4 LC1\",\n                \"Grade 5 Q2 LC10\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 31 and 33\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Solve multi-step problems involving multiplication, addition, and/or subtraction of decimals, including problems involving money.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Buying school supplies often takes more than one math step — multiply the price by the quantity, then add it all up!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Identify all the separate amounts involved in the problem (e.g., cost per item, number of items, other costs).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Do any needed multiplication first (price × quantity) for each item.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Add the results together to get a total.\",\n                  \"Step 4: If asked for change, subtract the total from the amount given.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Multi-step problems require identifying which operation to do first, second, and so on.\",\n                  \"Line up decimal points carefully when adding or subtracting money amounts.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"9 notebooks at ₱24.25 each and 4 pencils at ₱9.50 each — total cost?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"9 × 24.25 = 218.25. 4 × 9.50 = 38.00. Total = 218.25 + 38.00 = ₱256.25.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A ₱249.25 shirt and ₱179.25 shorts, paid with a ₱1,000 bill — how much change?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"249.25 + 179.25 = 428.50. 1,000.00 − 428.50 = ₱571.50 change.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"5 erasers at ₱6.50 each and 2 notebooks at ₱18.75 each — total cost?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Multiply each item's price by its quantity, then add the results.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"3 juice boxes at ₱15.50 each — total cost?\",\n                  \"A meal costs ₱185.50. If paid with a ₱200 bill, what is the change?\",\n                  \"6 pens at ₱12.25 each and 1 folder at ₱35.00 — total cost?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Planning a school project budget — buying multiple supplies and figuring out your remaining money — uses this exact process.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"4 notebooks at ₱22.50 each — total cost, and change from ₱100?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Multi-step decimal/money problems require multiplying first where needed, then adding or subtracting to find the final answer.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Plan a shopping list of 4 items with decimal prices, calculate the total, and find the change from ₱500.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_op_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Dividing Decimals\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q3 LC13b\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 35\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Divide a decimal of up to 2 decimal places by a 1- to 2-digit whole number, resulting in a terminating decimal quotient.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you need to split ₱5.68 equally among 8 people, how much does each person get? Decimal division helps!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Divide as you would with whole numbers, ignoring the decimal point at first.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Keep the decimal point in the quotient directly above the decimal point in the number being divided.\",\n                  \"Step 3: If needed, add zeros after the decimal point to continue dividing until there is no remainder.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Keep the decimal point aligned between the number being divided and the answer (quotient).\",\n                  \"You can add zeros after the decimal to keep dividing evenly.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"5.68 ÷ 8 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"8 goes into 56 seven times (56), remainder 0, bring down 8: 8 goes into 8 once. Quotient = 0.71.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"7.2 ÷ 4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Divide as with whole numbers, then place the decimal point correctly.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"9.6 ÷ 6 = ?\",\n                  \"12.4 ÷ 4 = ?\",\n                  \"3.15 ÷ 5 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Splitting a grocery bill of ₱248.50 equally among 5 friends uses decimal division.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"8.4 ÷ 12 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Dividing decimals follows the same steps as whole-number division, keeping the decimal point aligned in the quotient.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"45.6 ÷ 8 = ? (You may need to add a zero to finish dividing evenly.)\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_op_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Order of Operations (GMDAS)\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q4 LC2\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 39\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Perform three or more different operations by applying the GMDAS rules (Grouping, Multiplication/Division, Addition/Subtraction).\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If a math problem has many operations mixed together, which one do you do first? There's a rule for that!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: G — First, solve everything inside Grouping symbols (parentheses/brackets), starting with the innermost group.\",\n                  \"Step 2: MD — Next, perform Multiplication and Division, working from left to right.\",\n                  \"Step 3: AS — Finally, perform Addition and Subtraction, working from left to right.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Following this order every time guarantees everyone gets the same answer to the same problem.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"GMDAS order: Grouping symbols first, then Multiplication/Division (left to right), then Addition/Subtraction (left to right).\",\n                  \"Always solve the innermost grouping symbol first if there are multiple levels.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"500 − [(15 × 10) + (1/2 × 95)] = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Inside the brackets: 15×10=150, 1/2×95=47.5. 150+47.5=197.5. Then 500 − 197.5 = 302.5.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"(6 × 3) + (10 ÷ 2) = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Solve each grouped part first, then add.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"20 − (3 × 4) + 5 = ?\",\n                  \"(8 + 2) × (6 − 4) = ?\",\n                  \"100 − [(4 × 5) + (2 × 10)] = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Calculating a total bill with a discount and tax applied in the correct order uses GMDAS.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"(12 − 4) × 2 + 3 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"GMDAS tells you the order to solve mixed operations: Grouping first, then Multiplication/Division, then Addition/Subtraction.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"[(20 ÷ 4) + (3 × 3)] − (2 + 1) = ? Show each GMDAS step.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_op_04\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Probability of Simple Events\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q3 LC7\",\n                \"Grade 5 Q3 LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 30a and 30b\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Describe probability as a measure of the chance of an event occurring.\",\n                  \"Calculate the theoretical probability of a simple event by listing all possible outcomes.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If a box has more green marbles than blue ones, which color are you more likely to pick without looking?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Count the total number of possible outcomes (all items in the box).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Count how many of those outcomes match what you're looking for (the favorable outcomes).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Probability = favorable outcomes ÷ total outcomes. This can be written as a fraction, decimal, or percent.\",\n                  \"Step 4: The outcome with more favorable possibilities is 'more likely'; fewer possibilities makes it 'less likely.'\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Probability = number of favorable outcomes ÷ total number of outcomes.\",\n                  \"Probability can be expressed as a fraction, decimal, or percentage.\",\n                  \"Comparing probabilities tells you which event is more or less likely.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A box has 3 red, 2 blue, and 5 green marbles (10 total). What is the chance of picking blue?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"2 blue out of 10 total = 2/10 = 1/5 = 20% = 0.2.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Is it more or less likely to pick green than blue?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"5 green out of 10 vs. 2 blue out of 10 — since 5 > 2, it is more likely to pick green than blue.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A bag has 4 yellow and 6 blue balls. What is the probability of picking yellow?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Count favorable outcomes over total outcomes.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Is picking blue more or less likely than picking yellow in that same bag?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Compare 6 blue to 4 yellow.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A spinner has 8 equal sections: 3 red, 5 blue. What is the probability of landing on red?\",\n                  \"A jar has 10 marbles: 6 green, 4 red. Which color is more likely to be picked?\",\n                  \"Express a probability of 3/12 as a simplified fraction, a decimal, and a percent.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Weather forecasts using '70% chance of rain' are using probability to describe how likely rain is.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"A bag has 5 green and 5 red marbles. What is the probability of picking red?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Probability compares favorable outcomes to total outcomes, telling us how likely an event is to happen.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"A box has 2 red, 3 blue, 1 yellow, and 4 green marbles. Find the probability of picking each color, and rank them from least to most likely.\"\n                ]\n              },\n              \"moduleMappingNote\": \"The RMA source materials do not define a separate 'Data & Probability' module in the Mathalino Learn section. This lesson was placed in Operations because the RMA item requires the learner to compute or compare quantities (tallies, totals, differences, or chance expressed as a fraction) rather than reason abstractly about numbers (Number Sense) or shapes/measurement (Geometry). This is a placement judgment, not a category defined in the RMA materials themselves.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_op_05\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"operations\",\n              \"title\": \"Reading Line and Bar Graphs with Two Data Sets\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q3 LC6\",\n                \"Grade 5 Q3 LC4\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Items 37a, 37b, and 40\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Solve problems and interpret data using a double line graph or a double bar graph.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"A double line graph can compare boys and girls, or a double bar graph can compare singing and dancing — two stories in one picture!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Identify what each of the two lines or sets of bars represents (check the legend/key).\",\n                  \"Step 2: Read the value of each line/bar at the category you're asked about.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To compare, subtract the smaller value from the larger value, or check where the two lines meet (equal values).\",\n                  \"Step 4: To find the greatest total, add both values at each category and compare the totals.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A double line/bar graph compares two related sets of data across the same categories.\",\n                  \"Where two lines cross or meet, both data sets have an equal value at that point.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A double line graph shows boy and girl enrollees over 6 years. Which year has the greatest total enrollees?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Add the boy and girl values for each year, and compare the totals to find the greatest.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A double bar graph shows pupils who like singing vs. dancing. How many more pupils like dancing than singing?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Subtract the number who like singing from the number who like dancing.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A double bar graph shows Class A scored 45 and Class B scored 60 on a quiz. How many more points did Class B score?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Subtract Class A's score from Class B's score.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A double line graph shows sales of Product X and Product Y over 5 months. In which month were sales equal?\",\n                  \"A double bar graph compares boys' and girls' favorite sport. If 40 boys and 25 girls like basketball, how many more boys like it?\",\n                  \"Explain what it means when two lines on a double line graph cross at a point.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sports analysts use double bar graphs to compare two teams' scores across several games.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"If a double bar graph shows 30 for one category and 18 for another, what is the difference?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Double line and bar graphs let us compare two related data sets across the same categories, using subtraction or addition to answer comparison questions.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Create your own double bar graph idea (e.g., comparing two snack preferences across 3 classes) and describe what question it could answer.\"\n                ]\n              },\n              \"moduleMappingNote\": \"The RMA source materials do not define a separate 'Data & Probability' module in the Mathalino Learn section. This lesson was placed in Operations because the RMA item requires the learner to compute or compare quantities (tallies, totals, differences, or chance expressed as a fraction) rather than reason abstractly about numbers (Number Sense) or shapes/measurement (Geometry). This is a placement judgment, not a category defined in the RMA materials themselves.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_5_fractions\",\n          \"name\": \"Fractions\",\n          \"description\": \"Explore parts, wholes, and equivalent fractions.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_fr_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Converting Between Decimals and Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q2 LC6\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 29\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Convert terminating decimals to fractions, and vice versa.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"0.8 and 4/5 look different, but they represent the exact same amount!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: To convert a decimal to a fraction, write the decimal digits as the numerator, and use a power of 10 (10, 100, 1000...) matching the number of decimal places as the denominator.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Simplify the fraction if possible.\",\n                  \"Step 3: To convert a fraction to a decimal, divide the numerator by the denominator.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"0.8 = 8/10 = 4/5 (after simplifying).\",\n                  \"A fraction converts to a decimal by dividing the numerator by the denominator.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which shaded parts of a 5-part grid represent 0.8?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"0.8 = 8/10 = 4/5, so shading 4 out of 5 equal parts represents 0.8.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Convert 0.5 to a fraction in simplest form.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Write it as a fraction over 10, then simplify.\"\n                  },\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Convert 3/4 to a decimal.\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Divide the numerator by the denominator.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Convert 0.25 to a fraction in simplest form.\",\n                  \"Convert 1/2 to a decimal.\",\n                  \"Convert 0.6 to a fraction in simplest form.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Sports statistics like batting averages (0.300) are decimals that represent fractions of successful hits.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"Convert 0.75 to a fraction in simplest form.\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Decimals and fractions can represent the same value; convert between them using powers of 10 or division.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Convert 0.125 to a fraction in simplest form.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_fr_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Dividing Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q2 LC2\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 32\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Divide a fraction by a fraction, expressing the answer in simplest form.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"Dividing by a fraction sounds tricky, but there's a simple trick: flip and multiply!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: To divide by a fraction, keep the first fraction the same.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Flip (find the reciprocal of) the second fraction — swap its numerator and denominator.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Change the division sign to a multiplication sign, then multiply the two fractions.\",\n                  \"Step 4: Simplify your final answer.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"The reciprocal of a fraction flips its numerator and denominator (e.g., the reciprocal of 2/3 is 3/2).\",\n                  \"Dividing by a fraction = multiplying by its reciprocal.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"4/6 ÷ 2/3 = ?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Flip 2/3 to 3/2, then multiply: 4/6 × 3/2 = 12/12 = 1.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1/2 ÷ 1/4 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Flip the second fraction and multiply.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"3/5 ÷ 1/2 = ?\",\n                  \"2/3 ÷ 4/9 = ?\",\n                  \"5/8 ÷ 5/6 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"If you have 3/4 of a cup of flour and each recipe needs 1/8 cup, dividing fractions tells you how many recipes you can make.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"3/4 ÷ 1/2 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"To divide fractions, multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second, then simplify.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"7/10 ÷ 3/5 = ? Simplify your answer completely.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_fr_03\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"fractions\",\n              \"title\": \"Multiplying Fractions\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q1 LC8\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 38\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Multiply a fraction by a fraction.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If a recipe needs 3/4 cup of milk, but you only want to make half the recipe, how much milk do you need?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: To multiply fractions, multiply the numerators together to get the new numerator.\",\n                  \"Step 2: Multiply the denominators together to get the new denominator.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Simplify the resulting fraction if possible.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Multiply numerator × numerator, and denominator × denominator.\",\n                  \"'Of' in a word problem (like 'half of the recipe') often means multiply.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A recipe needs 3/4 cup of coconut milk. For 1/2 of the recipe, how much is needed?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"3/4 × 1/2 = 3/8 cup.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"1/3 × 2/5 = ?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Multiply numerators together, then denominators together.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"2/3 × 3/4 = ?\",\n                  \"1/2 × 1/2 = ?\",\n                  \"5/6 × 2/3 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Scaling a recipe up or down (like making 1/3 of the original amount) uses fraction multiplication.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"3/5 × 1/2 = ?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Multiply fractions by multiplying their numerators together and their denominators together, then simplify.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"4/5 × 5/8 = ? Simplify your final answer.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_5_geometry\",\n          \"name\": \"Geometry\",\n          \"description\": \"Learn shapes, space, measurement, and patterns.\",\n          \"topics\": [\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_geo_01\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Surface Area of Solid Figures\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q4 LC9\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 28\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Solve problems involving the surface area of solid figures.\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you wanted to wrap a birthday present, how would you know exactly how much wrapping paper to buy?\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: Surface area is the total area of every outside face of a solid shape added together.\",\n                  \"Step 2: For a rectangular box, find the area of each of the 6 faces (top, bottom, and 4 sides).\",\n                  \"Step 3: Since opposite faces are equal, you can find the area of 3 different faces and double each, then add them all.\",\n                  \"Step 4: The total is the least amount of material needed to fully cover the solid.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"Surface area = the sum of the areas of all faces of a solid.\",\n                  \"A rectangular box has 3 pairs of equal opposite faces.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A box is 9 cm × 6 cm × 5 cm. What is the least amount of wrapper needed?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Face areas: 9×6=54 (×2=108), 9×5=45 (×2=90), 6×5=30 (×2=60). Total = 108+90+60 = 258 cm².\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A box is 4 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm. What is its surface area?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Find the area of each pair of opposite faces, then add them all.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"A cube has sides of 5 cm each. What is its surface area?\",\n                  \"A box is 10 cm × 8 cm × 4 cm. What is its surface area?\",\n                  \"Explain why a box has 3 pairs of equal faces instead of 6 different ones.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Manufacturers calculate surface area to determine how much cardboard is needed to make a box.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"A box is 6 cm × 6 cm × 6 cm. What is its surface area?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"Surface area is the total area of all outside faces of a solid; for a box, find and add the areas of each pair of opposite faces.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"A box is 12 cm × 5 cm × 3 cm. Find its surface area and explain each step.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            },\n            {\n              \"id\": \"g5_geo_02\",\n              \"gradeLevel\": 5,\n              \"module\": \"geometry\",\n              \"title\": \"Solid Figures and Their Nets\",\n              \"rmaCompetencyCodes\": [\n                \"Grade 5 Q4 LC6\"\n              ],\n              \"rmaSource\": \"RMA_4-6 Answer Key, Item 36\",\n              \"lessonSequence\": {\n                \"objectives\": [\n                  \"Illustrate and describe solid figures and their nets (the flat pattern that folds into a solid).\"\n                ],\n                \"introduction\": \"If you flatten a cereal box, you get a net — a flat pattern that can be folded back into the 3D box!\",\n                \"explanation\": [\n                  \"Step 1: A net is a 2D (flat) pattern made of connected shapes.\",\n                  \"Step 2: When folded along its lines, a correct net forms a closed 3D solid, like a prism or pyramid, with no gaps or overlaps.\",\n                  \"Step 3: Not every flat pattern of shapes folds into a valid solid — some may leave gaps or extra overlapping faces.\",\n                  \"Step 4: To check a net, imagine folding each flap up and see if all the faces would meet correctly to close the shape.\"\n                ],\n                \"keyConcepts\": [\n                  \"A net is a flat pattern that folds into a 3D solid.\",\n                  \"A valid net for a prism must have the right number and arrangement of faces to close up completely.\"\n                ],\n                \"workedExamples\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"Which of 4 flat patterns would NOT form a prism when folded?\",\n                    \"solution\": \"Check each pattern for the correct number and arrangement of faces — the one that does not fold to properly close up as a prism is the answer.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"guidedPractice\": [\n                  {\n                    \"problem\": \"A net has 6 equal squares arranged in a cross/plus shape. What solid does it form when folded?\",\n                    \"hint\": \"Think about a familiar solid made of 6 equal square faces.\"\n                  }\n                ],\n                \"independentPractice\": [\n                  \"Sketch a net for a cube.\",\n                  \"Sketch a net for a rectangular prism (box).\",\n                  \"Explain why a net with a missing face would not form a closed solid.\"\n                ],\n                \"realLifeApplication\": \"Package designers create nets to figure out how a flat piece of cardboard can fold into a box shape.\",\n                \"checkUnderstanding\": [\n                  \"What is a 'net' in geometry?\"\n                ],\n                \"summary\": \"A net is a flat pattern of connected faces that folds into a closed 3D solid; not every arrangement of shapes forms a valid net.\",\n                \"challenge\": [\n                  \"Design your own net for a triangular prism and describe how many faces it needs.\"\n                ]\n              }\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"grade_6\",\n      \"gradeLevel\": 6,\n      \"status\": \"NOT_SUPPORTED_BY_SOURCE\",\n      \"note\": \"No competency in any attached RMA source document (Grade 1, Grade 2, or Grade 3 RMA2 booklets, the RMA_GRADE_4-6.docx test, or the RMA_4-6_ANSWERKEY.pdf) is explicitly labeled as Grade 6. The document titled 'RMA for GRADES 4 to 6' in fact only contains items explicitly labeled 'Grade 3', 'Grade 4', and 'Grade 5' competencies (see the 'Grade Level/Quarter/Page Number' column of the answer key, items 1-40). Per the project instructions, Grade 6 content is NOT invented here. The Mathalino_System_Architecture_and_Context_Document.pdf does describe general Grade 6 topics (four-quadrant geometry, ratios and proportions, GMDAS, surface area, probability, data interpretation), but that document is system/product context, not an RMA assessment source, so it has not been used to generate RMA-sourced lesson content here. ACTION NEEDED: supply a Grade 6 RMA instrument (or confirm which items, if any, are intended for Grade 6) before this module can be populated with formal lessons.\",\n      \"modules\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"grade_6_number_sense\",\n          \"name\": \"Number Sense\",\n          \"description\": \"Build confidence with numbers and place value.\",\n          \"topics\": [],\n          \"note\": \"No competency in any attached RMA source document (Grade 1, Grade 2, or Grade 3 RMA2 booklets, the RMA_GRADE_4-6.docx test, or the RMA_4-6_ANSWERKEY.pdf) is explicitly labeled as Grade 6. The document titled 'RMA for GRADES 4 to 6' in fact only contains it